      "A small group of words — a, an, the, this, these — are called articles or determiners; some grammars treat them as their own word class, others group them with adjectives, so don't worry if different books label them differently. What matters more for a beginner is this: many English words are not fixed to one part of speech. The same word can be a different part of speech depending on how it is used in the sentence. \"run\" is a verb in I run every day (it shows an action) but a noun in I went for a run (it names a thing). Checking a word's job in its own sentence, not just the word by itself, is the only reliable way to identify its part of speech.",
      "The most common mistake is confusing adjectives and adverbs, especially after a verb that describes how someone does something: She sings beautiful and He drives careful are both wrong, because \"beautiful\" and \"careful\" are adjectives, which describe nouns, not verbs. The sentence needs an adverb instead. Say She sings beautifully, He drives carefully. A second frequent error is guessing a word's part of speech only from its ending. The suffix -ly usually signals an adverb (quickly, slowly), but some adjectives also end in -ly (a friendly dog, a lovely evening), so the ending is only a clue, never a rule. Always check what the word is doing in its sentence.",
